
---
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import '../styles/global.css';
---

<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="utf-8" />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
		<title>About | Nuala King</title>
		<meta
			name="description"
			content="Nuala King is an Irish writer, artist and educator exploring landscape, mythology, ecological belonging and relational cosmologies."
		/>
	</head>

	<body>
		<Header />

		<main>
			<article>
				<h1>About</h1>

				<p class="intro">
					I am an Irish writer, artist and educator interested in
					the stories that shape our relationship with the world.
				</p>

				<p>
					I was raised among the rolling hills of Clarkhill and
					Legananny in County Down. The daughter of a storyteller,
					my early sense of place was shaped by my father's stories
					and my mother's deep connection to the landscape and
					community around them.
				</p>

				<p>
					Trained in architecture, I have long been interested in
					how the places we inhabit shape our sense of identity,
					belonging and our understanding of the world. My studies
					and work have taken me from Northern Ireland to Australia
					and back to Ireland, where I now live in North Tipperary.
				</p>

				<p>
					My writing brings together Irish mythology, folklore,
					landscape and ecology. I am drawn to the older stories
					that connect people to place, and to the possibility
					that these traditions might offer new ways of thinking
					about our responsibilities to the living world.
				</p>

				<p>
					My essay, <em>Holding the World Together: Myth, Language,
					and the Reawakening of Kanyini in the Irish Imagination</em>,
					appears in <em>Dúchas: Responses to Amitav Ghosh's
					Seamus Deane – Field Day Lecture</em> and in
					<em>Studies: An Irish Quarterly Review</em>.
				</p>

				<p>
					I continue to explore these questions through writing,
					art, traditional song and research into relational
					cosmologies: ways of understanding ourselves not as
					separate from nature, but as part of a wider web of
					life, place and relationship.
				</p>

				<p class="closing">
					This website is a place for writing, reflections and
					ongoing explorations of Irish culture, landscape and
					the stories that help us hold the world together.
				</p>
			</article>
		</main>

		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
	main {
		max-width: 760px;
		margin: 0 auto;
		padding: 4rem 1.5rem 6rem;
	}

	h1 {
		font-size: 2.8rem;
		font-weight: 400;
		margin-bottom: 2rem;
	}

	p {
		font-size: 1.08rem;
		line-height: 1.9;
		margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
	}

	.intro {
		font-size: 1.35rem;
		line-height: 1.7;
		margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
	}

	.closing {
		margin-top: 2.5rem;
		font-style: italic;
	}
</style>

